[{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_003274","candidate_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_003274","source_record_id":"rec_003274","source_corpus":"include","title":"Clinically aligned multi-modal image-text model for pan-cancer prognosis prediction","abstract":"Cancer prognosis prediction increasingly leverages multi-modal learning, yet existing approaches often rely on omics data that are costly and difficult to collect in routine practice. Pathology reports, by contrast, are routinely generated and widely available, providing a practical textual modality complementary to histology. We present\n\nCALM ( C linical A nchor text guided L earning for M ulti-modal prognosis prediction), a general framework that integrates pathology images and reports through riskspecific anchor texts. CALM systematically incorporates prior clinical knowledge by guiding image and text alignment with large language model-derived anchors, further refined via few-shot tuning. Across 14 TCGA cancer types, CALM improved prognostic accuracy compared to image and text baselines (up to +11.5% mean C-index), with enhanced training stability. CALM achieved performance comparable to image-omics integration in PORPOISE (0.652 vs. 0.644), highlighting the prognostic value of text. External validation in an independent head and neck cancer cohort demonstrated that CALM improved generalization in zero-shot settings. Attentionbased interpretation further confirmed that CALM aligns diagnostic text with tumor regions. Together, CALM offers a simple, interpretable, and clinically viable strategy for prognosis prediction, expanding the role of routine pathology reports as scalable priors in multi-modal oncology AI.\n\nKeywords Computational pathology, Multi-modality integration, Vision language modeling, Clinically aligned, Text anchoring","doi":"","year":"","venue":"","sources":[],"full_text_context":"[abstract: Abstract]\nCancer prognosis prediction increasingly leverages multi-modal learning, yet existing approaches often rely on omics data that are costly and difficult to collect in routine practice. Pathology reports, by contrast, are routinely generated and widely available, providing a practical textual modality complementary to histology. We present\n\nCALM ( C linical A nchor text guided L earning for M ulti-modal prognosis prediction), a general framework that integrates pathology images and reports through riskspecific anchor texts. CALM systematically incorporates prior clinical knowledge by guiding image and text alignment with large language model-derived anchors, further refined via few-shot tuning. Across 14 TCGA cancer types, CALM improved prognostic accuracy compared to image and text baselines (up to +11.5% mean C-index), with enhanced training stability. CALM achieved performance comparable to image-omics integration in PORPOISE (0.652 vs. 0.644), highlighting the prognostic value of text. External validation in an independent head and neck cancer cohort demonstrated that CALM improved generalization in zero-shot settings. Attentionbased interpretation further confirmed that CALM aligns diagnostic text with tumor regions. Together, CALM offers a simple, interpretable, and clinically viable strategy for prognosis prediction, expanding the role of routine pathology reports as scalable priors in multi-modal oncology AI.\n\nKeywords Computational pathology, Multi-modality integration, Vision language modeling, Clinically aligned, Text anchoring\n\n[introduction: Introduction]\nCancer remains one of the leading causes of death worldwide and continues to place heavy demands on healthcare systems. Despite major research efforts, improving cancer diagnosis and prognosis is still a major challenge. A key difficulty lies in the biological complexity and heterogeneity of cancer. These variations appear at many levels, from histopathological morphology to genomic and transcriptomic profiles, and extend across both tumor cells and their surrounding microenvironments [1]. Because of this complexity, accurate diagnosis and prognosis require more than pathological imaging of whole slide images (WSIs). Instead, they depend on integrating multiple clinical\n\n© The Author(s) 2025. Open Access This article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-NoDerivatives 4.0 International License, which permits any non-commercial use, sharing, distribution and reproduction in any medium or format, as long as you give appropriate credit to the original author(s) and the source, provide a link to the Creative Commons licence, and indicate if you modified the licensed material. You do not have permission under this licence to share adapted material derived from this article or parts of it. The images or other third party material in this article are included in the article's Creative Commons licence, unless indicated otherwise in a credit line to the material. [truncated]\n\n[discussion_conclusion: Discussion]\nIn this study, we introduced CALM, a strategy that leverages anchor texts to improve image and text based prognosis prediction in cancer. Unlike omics data, which require specialized assays with substantial costs and time, pathology reports are routinely generated and widely available in clinical practice. This makes text-guided multimodal learning a more practical and clinically translatable approach.\n\nOur results demonstrate that prior knowledge integration, originally applied in classification tasks, can be effectively extended to prognosis prediction. Importantly, we showed that refining anchors with few-shot tuning leads to substantial performance gains compared to using only general LLM-derived anchors. CALM improved across both uni-modal pathology encoders and multimodal vision-language settings, suggesting its broad applicability. Moreover, indirect comparison with omics-augmented models (e.g., PORPOISE) indicates that text can provide information complementary to histology at a level approaching omics.\n\nExternal validation under zero-shot conditions further highlighted CALM's robustness. Even with limited and imbalanced data, refined anchors improved generalization beyond baseline image and text models. Together, these findings suggest that CALM provides a simple, interpretable, and clinically viable alternative for integrating textual priors into pathology-based prognosis prediction.\n\nNonetheless, several limitations remain. [truncated]\n\n[data_representation: Dataset and processing]\nCALM was trained and validated using two datasets: (1) 14 cancer types from the public TCGA cohort (details provided in Appendix A) and (2) an in-house head and neck cancer (HNSC) dataset from Kyung Hee University Medical Center (KHMC).\n\nTCGA dataset For TCGA, we followed the setup of PORPOISE [18], using the same cancer types, patients, and publicly released split sets to ensure comparability. The data- set was divided into 80% training and 20% testing with 5-fold cross-validation at the patient level, using one slide per patient. Overall survival (OS) times were discretized within each cancer type into four quantile-based bins (25% each) and encoded as labels from 0 to 3, with higher labels corresponding to lower relative risk. Unlike PORPOISE, which did not employ a validation set, CALM reserved 20% of the training set for validation to enable early stopping and model checkpoint selection. OS was used as the primary outcome variable.\n\nFig. 1 Overview of CALM. CALM consists of three main components: an image encoding block, a text encoding block, and a cross-attention fusion block. Risk-specific anchor texts (high-, intermediate-, and low-risk) are employed to establish clearer decision boundaries between risk strata, thereby improving training stability and predictive performance\n\n<!-- image --> [truncated]\n\n[data_representation: preprocessing]\nTo ensure consistency and reproducibility, we employed the TRIDENT pipeline [29] for WSI preprocessing and feature extraction. TRIDENT provides standardized functions for patch extraction and feature generation. Tissue segmentation was performed using GrandQC, and all patch analyses were conducted at 20× magnification (0.5 mpp). Default TRIDENT parameters were used unless otherwise specified. Patch size and preprocessing settings followed encoder-specific recommendations: 384 × 384 for MUSK [3] and 256 × 256 for UNI v1 [11].\n\n[data_representation: Text report preprocessing]\nTCGA pathology reports are originally handwritten and digitized, often lengthy and complex, necessitating preprocessing. We utilized the dataset released by TCGAReports [30], which applied optical character recognition (OCR) to convert scanned reports into text. Since the extracted reports often exceeded model context length, we adopted structured summarization following MUSK [3]. For summarization, we used the LLaMA 3.1 70B Instruct-tuned model [31]. Among the two text encoders in this study, MUSK-L supports a maximum context length of 100 tokens, whereas BioMedBERT [32] supports 512 tokens. Accordingly, we designed two strategies: (1) short-context summarization and (2) long-context summarization. For cases in which pathology reports were unavailable, we inserted a placeholder sentence stating 'Text report is not available. '\n\n#### Short-context summarization\n\nSince the MUSK-L encoder is limited to 100 tokens, MUSK adopted a structured prompt consisting of five morphology-related questions [3]. This approach generates a concise structural summary that can serve as model input. To summarize large-scale pathology reports, we followed structured prompts proposed by MUSK. The five categories are as follows: [truncated]\n\n[data_representation: Does CALM generalize to in-house datasets?]\nTo assess the generalizability of CALM to in-house data, we evaluated it on the KHMC-HNSC cohort. We compared MUSK-based CALM against several baselines, including image-only, text-only, and image and text models. Given the limited sample size and severe class imbalance, we performed zero-shot inference-based evaluation rather than full fine-tuning on the KHMC-HNSC dataset. Specifically, we employed five models pretrained on TCGA-HNSC and tested them directly on the entire KHMCHNSC cohort.","section_evidence":[{"section_type":"abstract","heading":"Abstract","heading_level":3,"line_start":17,"line_end":24,"text":"Cancer prognosis prediction increasingly leverages multi-modal learning, yet existing approaches often rely on omics data that are costly and difficult to collect in routine practice. Pathology reports, by contrast, are routinely generated and widely available, providing a practical textual modality complementary to histology. We present\n\nCALM ( C linical A nchor text guided L earning for M ulti-modal prognosis prediction), a general framework that integrates pathology images and reports through riskspecific anchor texts. CALM systematically incorporates prior clinical knowledge by guiding image and text alignment with large language model-derived anchors, further refined via few-shot tuning. Across 14 TCGA cancer types, CALM improved prognostic accuracy compared to image and text baselines (up to +11.5% mean C-index), with enhanced training stability. CALM achieved performance comparable to image-omics integration in PORPOISE (0.652 vs. 0.644), highlighting the prognostic value of text. External validation in an independent head and neck cancer cohort demonstrated that CALM improved generalization in zero-shot settings. Attentionbased interpretation further confirmed that CALM aligns diagnostic text with tumor regions. Together, CALM offers a simple, interpretable, and clinically viable strategy for prognosis prediction, expanding the role of routine pathology reports as scalable priors in multi-modal oncology AI.\n\nKeywords Computational pathology, Multi-modality integration, Vision language modeling, Clinically aligned, Text anchoring","original_chars":1562},{"section_type":"introduct